sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Successive minimal-area divisions of a disc soon leave only quadrilateral cells at the periphery, which divide radially, producing a superficial layer of parallel-sided cells (an epidermis) contrasting with the irregular cells beneath.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Successive minimal-area divisions leave quadrilateral cells forming an epidermis";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Origin of an epidermis by minimal-area division”. Scope: discoid and rounded embryonic structures of plants (moss embryos, algal discs, antheridia)"
}
